 ## Overview
-Refractive Index Tomography using Neural Fields (RNTF) is a method to recover a 3D refractive index field ($\eta$) and a luminance field from single-view refracted image measurements by optimizing neural fields that represent these quantities [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard differentiable volume rendering by modeling light propagation through a continuous medium with spatially varying refractive index using ray tracing governed by Fermat's principle, rather than assuming straight-line paths or constant index [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Physical and Material Priors](11198-physical-and-material-priors.md) by explicitly parameterizing the material property (refractive index) as a neural field and integrating the physical equations of motion for light rays.
+Refractive Index Tomography using Neural Fields (RNTF) recovers a 3D refractive index field $\eta(\mathbf{x})$ and a luminance field from single-view refracted image measurements by optimizing neural fields that represent these quantities [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard differentiable volume rendering by modeling light propagation through a continuous medium with spatially varying refractive index using ray tracing governed by Fermat's principle, rather than assuming straight-line paths or constant index [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Physical and Material Priors](11198-physical-and-material-priors.md) by explicitly parameterizing the material property (refractive index) as a neural field and integrating the physical equations of motion for light rays [S1, unofficial].
 
